--TEST--
`Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::constants` names every constant a class declares and `Core\Reflect\ConstantInfo` carries the visibility of each and whether its value folded — `rule:core-classes/reflect` and `rule:types/constant-in-type-position`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::constants
// covers: Core\Reflect\ConstantInfo::name
// covers: Core\Reflect\ConstantInfo::isPublic
// covers: Core\Reflect\ConstantInfo::hasValue
// `rule:core-classes/reflect`: naming a constant reads the program's *shape* and
// is always available; reading its value is acting, and that is
// `Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::constant`'s. So the roster is complete — the
// `private` constant is here with its bit saying so, exactly as the property
// roster carries a `private` property.
class Limits {
    public const int MAX = 10;
    private const string KEY = "ada";
    public const array ROWS = [1, 2];
}

var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Limits());
var $roster = $described->constants();

// All three, declaration order. The count is asserted beside the names because
// a roster that dropped the `private` one still prints two plausible lines.
echo Core\Arr::count($roster), "\n";
foreach ($roster as Core\Reflect\ConstantInfo $constant) {
    echo $constant->name(), " ", $constant->isPublic() ? "public" : "closed", " ",
         $constant->hasValue() ? "value" : "none", "\n";
}

// `rule:types/constant-in-type-position`: Novis folds a constant that *is* a literal and runs no
// second constant-expression evaluator, so an `array` constant has no
// compile-time value to hand back. `hasValue` reports that stated bound ahead
// of the call rather than leaving a caller to discover it from a throw — and
// it is `false` for exactly the constants the checker also refuses in type
// position.
echo $described->constant("MAX") as int, "\n";
--EXPECT--
3
MAX public value
KEY closed value
ROWS public none
10
